{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And he dreamed.</b> This dream is not like the dreams customary among human beings, nor is it merely a dream of the holy spirit (ru'aḥ ha-kodesh); for were that so, it would have said at the end, 'And Jacob awoke from his sleep, and behold it was a dream.' Rather, it is a dream of complete prophecy, like the dreams of the prophets whose prophecy is of two types, these being what is written (Numbers 12:6): 'במראה אליו אתודע בחלום אדבר בו' — 'In a vision I make Myself known to him; in a dream I speak with him.'" "<b>And behold, a ladder set up on the earth.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): in this vision he saw that the feet of the ladder were in Be'er-sheva, the place where he was lying, which is what it says, 'set up on the earth.' And the top of the ladder rested and cleaved to heaven opposite the Temple site — this is what it says, 'and its top reaching heavenward,' meaning the aperture through which the ministering angels enter and exit, as it says, 'and behold, angels of Elohim going up and coming down.' And the Holy One, blessed be He, was standing upon it; then Jacob knew that the place where he lay was the gate of heaven and was fit for prayer. And the Temple is the house of Elohim — that is the main thing, for the top of the ladder was opposite it and the Holy One, blessed be He, was standing upon it. Therefore he said, 'This is none other than the house of Elohim, and this is the gate of heaven' — 'this is the house of the Holy One, blessed be He' opposite which the top of the ladder reaches and cleaves to heaven, and 'this is the gate of heaven' is Be'er-sheva, where the feet of the ladder were. And so they said in the Midrash: Rabbi Eliezer said in the name of R. Yosi ben Zimra: this ladder stood in Be'er-sheva, and its slope reached as far as the Temple. What it says, 'ושפועו' (ve-shif'o, its slope) — the end of its slope, which is the top of the ladder, reaches as far as the Temple, meaning opposite the Temple. And the intent thereof is that it was cleaving to heaven opposite the Temple." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'This ladder is Sinai; its numerical value is the numerical value of this' (סלם סיני = 130 each). 'Set up on the earth,' as in what is written (Exodus 19:17): 'ויתיצבו בתחתית ההר' — 'and they stationed themselves at the foot of the mountain.' 'And its top reaching heavenward,' as in what is written (Deuteronomy 4:11): 'וההר בוער באש עד לב השמים' — 'and the mountain burned with fire to the heart of heaven.' 'And behold, angels of Elohim' — this is Moses and Aaron, as it is said (Exodus 19:3): 'ומשה עלה אל האלהים' — 'and Moses went up to Elohim.' And it is written: 'וירד משה' and so on, 'ואל הזקנים אמר' — 'and Moses descended ... and to the elders he said.' 'And behold, YHWH,' as it is said (Exodus 19:20): 'וירד ה׳ על הר סיני' — 'and YHWH descended upon Mount Sinai.' 'I am YHWH, the Elohim of Abraham your father,' as it is said (Exodus 20:2): 'אנכי ה׳ אלהיך' — 'I am YHWH your Elohim.'" "<b>And in the chapters of R. Eliezer.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, showed him four kingdoms — their rule and their destruction. He showed him the prince of the kingdom of Babylon ascending seventy rungs and descending; he showed him the prince of the kingdom of Media ascending fifty-two rungs and descending; he showed him the prince of the kingdom of Greece ascending one hundred and eighty rungs and descending; he showed him the prince of the kingdom of Edom ascending and not descending, and saying (Isaiah 14:15): 'אעלה על במתי עב אדמה לעליון' — 'I will ascend upon the heights of the cloud; I will be like the Most High.' Jacob said to him: 'אך אל שאול תורד' — 'yet you shall be brought down to Sheol' and so on. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him (Obadiah 1:4): 'אם תגביה כנשר' — 'though you soar aloft like the eagle' and so on." "<b>According to this Midrash</b>, this prophetic dream contains a promise to Israel that they will return on high at the end of all the exile. For all the patriarchs, each one, saw in a prophetic vision everything that would befall their seed to the end of all generations. And just as the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Abraham, in the covenant between the pieces, the matter of the four kingdoms — which is what the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'והנה אימה זו' (behold, a dread) — this is the kingdom of Babylon; 'חשכה' (darkness) — this is the kingdom of Media; 'גדולה' (great) — this is the kingdom of Greece; 'נופלת עליו' (falling upon him) — this is the kingdom of Edom — so too He showed Jacob in this vision the dominion of the four kingdoms, their ascent and their descent; for they have princes on high, and each one leads his nation below, as it is written in Daniel (Daniel 10:13): 'ושר מלכות יון' — 'and the prince of the kingdom of Greece' — 'ושר מלכות פרס עומד לנגדי' — 'and the prince of the kingdom of Persia stands against me.' This is what is mentioned here: 'and behold, angels of Elohim going up and coming down on it' — for they are the angels, the princes of the kingdoms. And Scripture assures us of their descent after the ascent." "<b>And in Bereshit Rabbah.</b> 'And behold, the angels of Elohim' — those of Sodom, which had been banished from their domain and afterwards ascended; and now they descend in it to go hence on missions of the Place (המקום). Another interpretation: 'And behold, the angels of Elohim' — those that had escorted him from the land of Israel ascend, and others descend to escort him outside the land. 'Ascending and descending' — they ascend above and see the likeness of Jacob engraved upon the Throne of Glory; they descend below and find him sleeping." "<b>And by way of reason (sekhel).</b> The matter of the ladder is an allusion to existence. And since a ladder has many rungs, one above another, therefore Scripture likens existence to it, because existence is divided into three parts: the part of the angels, the part of the spheres, and the part of the lower world. So too David, peace be upon him, arranged them: ״ברכו ה' מלאכיו״ — 'Bless HaShem, O His angels' (Psalms 103:20); ״ברכו ה' כל צבאיו״ — 'Bless HaShem, all His hosts' (Psalms 103:21); ״ברכו ה' כל מעשיו״ — 'Bless HaShem, all His works' (Psalms 103:22). And so Solomon arranged them in the Song of Songs, in his wisdom, in three consecutive verses: the first for His cheeks, the second for His hands, the third for His legs. For Knesset Israel recounts the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He, through the parts of the creatures He created. 'His cheeks' (לחייו) — these are the angels who convey prophecy to the prophets, and he likened them to ערוגת הבושם, a bed of spices, for spices have changing hues, white and red; so too the angels — some are of water and some of fire. 'His lips' (שפתותיו) are the prophets who proclaim to the people, 'and his tongue' — the tongue of prophecy, as in the language of (Ezekiel 21:2): ״והטף אל דרום״ — 'and drop toward the south'; and he likened prophecy to myrrh, in which the soul delights. 'His hands' (ידיו) — these are the spheres, and this is in the language of גלילי, and as it says (Psalms 8:4): ״כי אראה שמיך״ — 'when I behold Your heavens'; and he likened them to hands because just as all the actions of a person depend on them, so all the operations of the entire world are drawn from the spheres. ממולאים בתרשיש — filled with tarshish, for the spheres in their thickness and their hollow are filled with stars, which He called tarshish, which is superior to gold, for thus the stars' excellence is greater than that of the spheres. 'His abdomen' (מעיו) — עשת שן — these are the twelve constellations; this is what it says מעולפת ספירים, that is, twelve sapphires. 'His legs' (שוקיו) — this is the lower world, the end of existence, for thus the legs are the end of the body, and 'his legs' is said of fire and of wind, while אדני פז — bases of gold — are the waters and the dust. And all this existence is here called a ladder." "<b>And the Holy One, blessed be He, made known</b> to Jacob that He, exalted be He, stands upon this ladder and governs all three parts of existence and watches over all of them. And He began from this lower world in which he stood and which was near to him, arranging and proceeding from below upward: ״מוצב ארצה״ — 'set upon the earth' — this is an allusion to the lower world; ״וראשו מגיע השמימה״ — 'and its top reaches heaven' — that is the world of the spheres, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 19:2): ״השמים מספרים כבוד אל״ — 'the heavens recount the glory of El,' and the intent is the spheres in their motion. ״והנה מלאכי אלהים״ — 'and behold, the angels of Elohim' — that is the world of angels, which is the third part. And from this our Rabbis derived: 'the angel is a third of the world' — it should not be understood as a third of the world in length and breadth, but rather that it is the third of the entire world, a third part; thus the Rav explained in the perashah of Terumah regarding the matter of the cherubim. And it says ״עולים ויורדים״ — 'ascending and descending' — an allusion to their perdurance and their cessation, for Scripture calls perdurance 'ascent' and cessation 'descent,' and it comes to allude that there are angels that endure eternally forever — those created on the second day — and there are among them those that cease, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: each and every day the Holy One, blessed be He, creates a host of angels, and they recite song and pass away — these are those created on the fifth day. And the word בו, 'in it,' refers back to the ladder, by which Scripture likened existence; ״והנה ה' נצב עליו״ — 'and behold, HaShem stood upon it' — for the Holy One, blessed be He, is supreme over all, pouring His power into the angels, and the angels pour power into the spheres, and the spheres in their motion pour power into the lower beings. You find thereby that the cause of every operation in the lower realm is the spheres, and the cause of the spheres is the angels, and the cause of the angels is YHWH, exalted be He, whose providence flows upon all. And this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Ecclesiastes 5:7): ״אם עשק רש וגזל משפט וצדק תראה במדינה״ — 'if you see oppression of the poor and perversion of justice and righteousness in the province' — that is, in the lower world — ״אל תתמה על החפץ״ — 'do not marvel at the matter,' and do not think that it is crooked justice; not so — for the judgment is true and the ways of HaShem, blessed be He, are higher than our ways and His thoughts than our thoughts, for His providence is over all. This is (ibid.): ״כי גבוה מעל גבוה שומר וגבוהים עליהם״ — 'for high above the high one watches, and higher ones over them' — that is the Holy One, blessed be He, who is supreme over all, and He encompasses all the heights, all of them. And this is why he used a plural locution, and it is in the noun אלהים, which is so called in the plural form; and likewise ״אלהים קדושים הוא״ — for it is written (Joshua 24:19): ״כי אלהים קדושים הוא״ — 'for holy Elohim is He' — because He encompasses all the powers and all the sanctities." "<b>And know</b> the opinion of the Rambam regarding the matter of the ladder, that he called the totality of the spheres by the name 'ladder.' And he said מוצב ארצה — 'set upon the earth' — because its first head was in the earth, that is, the beginning of the elements; and ״וראשו מגיע השמימה״ — 'its top reaches heaven' — which is the 'aravot, as in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 33:26): ״רוכב שמים״ — 'Rider of heavens' — and there is the entire Chariot; and on this ladder whoever ascends shall ascend. And מלאכי אלהים, 'the angels of Elohim,' are the prophets, in the manner of what is written (Numbers 20:16): ״וישלח מלאך״ — 'and He sent an angel' — and it is written of Haggai (Haggai 1:13): ״מלאך ה'״ — 'the angel of HaShem' — and behold, they ascend there by the rank of their prophecy up to known degrees, and afterwards they descend, for they make their prophecy known and teach the creatures the way of HaShem." "<b>This</b> he wrote at the beginning of his book, the Guide, chapter 15. But in chapter 10 of the second part he wrote another matter on this, and he prefaced it first, that the spheres are comprised under the four elements: the sphere of the moon moves the element of water, and the sphere of the sun moves the element of fire, and the sphere of the five wandering stars — the remaining planets — moves the element of wind, and the eighth sphere, which is the sphere of the constellations and the fixed stars, moves the element of earth." "<b>And behold.</b> They are one element (יסוד) and one sphere (גלגל), and he said that henceforth one must contemplate that which our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded — that there were four virtues in the ladder. That is to say: since all the spheres are included in the four, and they move the four elements." "<b>And he appeared.</b> According to his words in chapter 10, this — which Jacob attained, from the beginning of the elements unto the 'Aravot' — is what is meant by 'a ladder set up on the earth, and its head reaches the heaven': that is, the encompassing sphere, which is the 'Aravot, upon which HaShem, blessed be He, stands. And 'the angels of Elohim' — it appears that his intent is upon the elements, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 104:4) ״עושה מלאכיו רוחות״ — 'He makes His angels winds' — the two ascending above and the two descending below. And the reason he mentioned the descent of the spheres first and afterward the ascent — for he said, 'and behold, HaShem stood above it' — so too we find (Ezekiel 1) that he first mentioned the holy living creatures (חיות הקודש), which are the angels, and then he descended to the Ofanim, which are the spheres according to all the commentators, and the elements according to the Rambam, of blessed memory; and afterward he ascended and mentioned an attainment superior to both of them, namely the attainment of the חשמל (hashmal)." "<b>And a proof.</b> For the opinion of the Rav, of blessed memory, that the Ofanim are the elements: the language of the verse (ibid.) ״והנה אופן אחד בארץ״ — 'and behold one Ofan on the earth' — that is, joined to the earth, for so we find that the elements are called by the name 'earth.' And further, that he mentioned their strength and their actions, to indicate that they receive action; and further, that he mentioned in them their flesh and their backs, and the expression 'their flesh' likewise indicates this." "<b>And the sage.</b> Ibn Tibbon, of blessed memory, wrote that this ladder alludes to the quality of apprehension that the human species can attain, until it be perfected in him. And he began by informing first of that path, and afterward informed of its culmination. And he informed that that path is not a path paved on the earth, neither in its length nor in its breadth, but it is a path of ascent, and its head is in the heavens, as it says (Proverbs 15:24) ״ארח חיים למעלה למשכיל״ — 'the path of life is upward for the wise.' (See his book 'Yakav ha-Mayim,' chapter 11)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {6 "Rabbeinu Bahya reads Song of Songs 5:13–15 as an allegorical anatomy mapping the three tiers of existence: לחייו/שפתותיו = angels and prophets; ידיו/מעיו = spheres and constellations; שוקיו/אדניו = the lower world's elements. Quotations from Song of Songs are woven into the argument without explicit citation; the verse references are implicit.", 8 "Rambam's interpretation is given in Guide for the Perplexed I:15; the following comment (index 4) notes that Rambam offers a different interpretation in II:10.", 13 "The text reads בן תבון; resolved as Ibn Tibbon, per the cross-reference to his work 'Yakav ha-Mayim' (a philosophical work by Jacob Ibn Tibbon, also known as Prophatius)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:43.782638383Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}